<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A second synthetic footprint, drawn as a skull.

  Nothing here is real, and the shape is a joke rather than a project. It earns
  its place because the drawing exercises the import harder than the first
  example does, in one file:

    - two disjoint polygons, the cranium and the lower jaw, which the model
      treats as one footprint made of two parcels. They are separated by open
      ground, so they tip independently and neither borrows support from the
      other;
    - several holes in one polygon: two eye sockets, a nasal cavity, and the
      gaps between the teeth. The teeth gaps are cut through the bottom edge,
      so they arrive as notches rather than as enclosed holes;
    - a deeply concave outline, so a large share of the households are boundary
      households. At the default 50 m cell, 360 of the 1056 households sit on
      the perimeter, 34%, against 28% for the first example; at a 100 m cell the
      perimeter becomes the majority at 55%. The sockets and the teeth gaps are
      interior edges, and the model counts them exactly like the outer one.

  Coordinates place it on land near Niamey so that a degree of longitude has a
  realistic scale. About 2 km across, so 40 x 45 cells at the default 50 m per
  cell. The face reads clearly at 50 m and still at 80 m; by 100 m the teeth
  have turned into noise, though the sockets survive.
-->
